  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "EkgtkgtVKPOAx1It3k0l2j+0N9w=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:26:05.952Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "Indra/Minsait is excluded on size and posture together. The size is recorded precisely \u2014 62,396 people \u2014 which places it several orders of magnitude beyond the firms the screen treats as workable first partners. The posture is legible from its stack: Microsoft, SAP, Salesforce, AWS and IBM, a portfolio of proprietary vendor alliances with no open-source anchor.\n\nEither criterion alone would be enough. At this headcount any engagement is an enterprise procurement with a committee, a vendor-management function and an alliance team that scores new technologies against existing partner commitments. And a portfolio built entirely on proprietary alliances means the reference architecture a Powerhouse layer would join is one where client-owned, open infrastructure is not the default.\n\nThe note is terse because the verdict needs no nuance: it is not a near miss whose ranking could shift on new evidence, but a structural mismatch on the two attributes the screen weights first.\n\n---\n\n## Leads sharing this finding (1)\n\n**9 \u00b7 do not pitch**\n- Indra / Minsait (ES \u00b7 62,396)\n\nVerbatim research text this claim was extracted from:\n\n> Ruled out on size and posture.",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-19T11:26:05.952667Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:26:05.952671Z"
    }
  },
